From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 05:12:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] LINE Slips: pull GL live from the dashboard, always in sync
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The LINE Slips module kept its own cash book in localStorage (or a remote
backend) with no link to the GL the dashboard already holds, and its page
container and sidebar tab had been dropped from the build, so the module
was unreachable.

Restore the module and wire it to the dashboard's own GL data.

Module restored
- sidebar tab "LINE Slips · Cash" under Finance & Reconcile
- <div id="page-line"> container next to the other module pages
- MOD_META entry, so the header/description renders and the module can be
  restored as the last-opened one on reload

GL Auto-Link panel (new lineGlCss + lineGlScript)
Reads GLROWS / GLMETA / GLACTIVE live on every render — no copy, no second
store — so what the module shows is exactly the GL the Database module is
holding. Six tabs, filtered by year/month:
- balances of every cash & bank account (A/C 111x, 112x): brought forward,
  received, paid, closing; plus slip-book vs GL variance per account
- reconciliation LINE slips <-> GL: matched / slip-without-GL /
  GL-without-slip, matching on direction + amount (0.01) + date tolerance
- GL cash movement with search and CSV export
- cash flow by Ref. Code (project), named from Project Master
- input/output VAT and WHT payable straight from the GL accounts
- mapping of each LINE bank to its GL account, auto-guessed by bank name

Staying current
- wraps window.renderLine so the panel is repainted with the page
- hooks _glAfterIngest for an instant refresh on a new GL import
- polls the GL signature (active file + row count + import timestamp) every
  2s, covering file switches, cloud sync and the embedded-GL boot, and
  repaints the panel if the module re-rendered without it

Writing back is explicit, never automatic: set LINE opening balances from
GL, and import unmatched GL entries into the slip book (deduplicated by GL
key, reversible in one click).

